  "analytic_review_groups": [
    "Fixed ordinary-model setting distinguishes scalar uniqueness from potential uniqueness; smooth gauge transport preserves the original current and prescribed type.",
    "Scalar order uses strict domination of every constant translate; mixed bounded products give logarithmic concavity in the unchanged ordinary type.",
    "Uniformly bounded logarithmic densities supply scalar bounds, a common relative barrier, capacity convergence and the limiting equation under L1 perturbations.",
    "Hahn--Banach directional support is positive and has mass one; Riesz representation and null-set cutoffs prove absolute continuity.",
    "Directional derivative extrema have the correct right/left signs; upper semicontinuous widths, countable line corners and Baire category give generic uniqueness.",
    "Smooth Gauduchon recovery follows from signed differentiation; the one-dimensional Stokes formula is treated separately. General continuous supporting uniqueness remains open.",
    "The explicit divisorial model has slopes one and two and satisfies the three-quarter smaller-background condition; bounded residuals have mass four.",
    "Divisorial cutoffs and bounded closed products exclude divisor mass; the residual cofactor uses residual mass, not the original theta trace.",
    "Rank-one smoothing decomposition is positive; normal rescaled kernels have masses one and one-half, giving a divisor probability of one-half with smoothing-dependent tangential term.",
    "Finite Lp convergence of logarithmic densities does not give uniform integrability of exponential densities. The prose now states the uniform logarithmic bound required by the cited theorem.",
    "Fixed-divisor bounded residuals give the stated capacity error and weak current/probability recovery; globally smooth fillings are outside this regime.",
    "Convex dual minima are exactly supporting probabilities; fixed-height cutoffs give uniform small-set control on finite sublevels, and Lusin approximation upgrades weak measures to weak L1 densities.",
    "Bounded measurable tests and simultaneous density/measure limits use uniform integrability; infinite dual objectives and almost-everywhere representatives are explicitly well defined.",
    "Entropy variational formula covers singular measures, infinite entropy, clipped logarithms and nonunit reference volumes.",
    "Hermitian dual estimates use the Lq density lower bound and Gauduchon trace upper bound; dimension one and uniform finite-domain assertions retain their own hypotheses.",
    "Entropy differences recover both mass extrema using smooth Gibbs probabilities, smooth adjoints and exact bounded regularization, including zero and infinite extrema.",
    "Prescribed-type entropy constants are uniform only for fixed theta and the smaller-background parameter; compactness alone does not identify the limiting support set.",
    "Reference order and quantitative lower bounds use a smaller-background competitor and finite-level positivity off its polar set; dual inequalities retain infinite values.",
    "The scalar comparison is a pseudometric, equals the finite-entropy dual comparison and extends to bounded measurable tests; approximate minima have error at most twice the scalar distance.",
    "Two-sided inequalities between positive forms give determinant comparisons, smooth gauge invariance and weak L1 supporting stability.",
    "Smooth background/potential convergence uses the parameter-dependent implicit function theorem and adjoint Fredholm mean; functional reconstruction integrates the derivative along smooth tests.",
    "Divisorial removal identifies bounded residual ordinary types and all one-sided classes canonically, even with additional poles or infinite mass. The separately read bounded measurable positive-density corollary supplies uniqueness.",
    "Moving sections use fixed line bundles and metrics, nonzero limits, positive limiting weights and either fixed residual bounded-data compactness or smooth variable-residual convergence.",
    "The genuine divisorial model follows from submean bounds and a smaller-background competitor; all three restricted smooth thresholds agree and nonzero divisors cause strict scalar loss by the elliptic maximum principle.",
    "No smooth-data critical subsolution has a divisorial component; product loss calculations retain the zero-lower-mass factor and do not close an arbitrary individual-density question.",
    "Closed model mass quotients retain small unbounded locus and positive mass. Alternating divisors show identical functionals/supports with distinct ordinary/ideal types and distinct potential clusters.",
    "Toric Legendre envelopes have the stated canonical models, translated-simplex barrier and Alexandrov mass; blowup descent and the incidence embedding justify the geometric background.",
    "Common inradius and Hausdorff comparison give volume bounds; common integrable lower barriers give reference L1 convergence, while exponential probabilities converge in total variation.",
    "Curved isolated poles are nonanalytic by their non-piecewise-linear directional coefficient; corner integration gives positive endpoint mass. Degenerate residuals have rank one on a different exceptional divisor and mass 1-t^n.",
    "Vanishing selection uses a lattice norm without claiming its completeness, a positive norming face, and weighted moments obtained with q<p. Probabilities exclude the zero forcing set and analytic complement.",
    "Centered potentials retain the scalar factor in density bounds. Pointwise forcing envelopes need no attainment; weak limiting supports, portmanteau truncations and fixed-face moment continuity prove greatest selected limit and its exact mean normalization.",
    "Logarithmic product models use the full competitor family. One-sided removal and bounded exponential comparison give greatest solutions; Green contraction and logarithmic kernel tails give joint residual limits at unrestricted relative rates.",
    "Logarithmic scalar centering follows the moving critical scalar and its first moment; separated critical order is not uniqueness of arbitrary critical potentials.",
    "Continuous residual weak adjoints use the signed heat resolvent and harmonic product tests on full C(X); uniform residual approximations recover weights/currents weakly without pointwise weight bounds.",
    "Radial density inversion uses positive cumulative central mass, smooth positive roots across density zeros and a smooth inverse-radius tail; relative density error controls two residual derivatives without an error-derivative hypothesis.",
    "Critical radial C1/C2 thresholds and Holder exponents are sharp; flat errors and compensated zero annuli preserve unit mass and the genuine pole. Product masses retain their factor hypothesis.",
    "The singular radial envelope is the full unitary-invariant convex minorant. Free intervals, differentiable contact, left tangents and the two infinity alternatives give C1,1 residuals and unit non-pluripolar mass.",
    "Fixed obstacle support and C2 bounds give a common pole neighborhood and weighted infinity Hessian control through moving contact; interpolation gives C1,gamma convergence and no C1,1 convergence claim.",
    "Full Hermitian product restrictions are justified by corrected decreasing convolution and vanishing curvature error; dimensional truncations give the exact product measure. The Hopf fourfold stays nonclosed and non-Kahler."
  ],
